      <h2 id="sources-heading">What we look at for each source</h2>
      <p>The process is the same; the details are not. These are the areas that need the most care for each system.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid" data-stagger>
      <article class="card detail" id="salesforce">
        <h3>Salesforce to HubSpot migration</h3>
        <p>Salesforce orgs usually carry years of custom objects, record types, validation rules, and automation. We map each object and record type to a HubSpot object and pipeline, decide which custom objects are still needed, and rebuild the logic in HubSpot workflows rather than copying it blindly.</p>
        <ul><li>Leads, contacts, accounts, and opportunities mapped to HubSpot objects</li><li>Record types translated into pipelines or properties</li><li>Owners, activities, and associations carried across where they matter</li><li>Flows and assignment rules rebuilt and tested in a sandbox</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail" id="pipedrive">
        <h3>Pipedrive to HubSpot migration</h3>
        <p>Pipedrive data is deal-centric, so the work is mostly about giving it a fuller structure in HubSpot: lifecycle stages, company records, and pipelines with clear exit criteria, instead of a straight copy of deal stages.</p>
        <ul><li>Deals, persons, organizations, and activities mapped</li><li>Pipelines redesigned around how deals actually close</li><li>Custom fields consolidated into a governed property catalogue</li><li>Activities and notes kept with their deals and contacts</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail" id="zoho">
        <h3>Zoho to HubSpot migration</h3>
        <p>Zoho CRM setups often combine modules, custom fields, and workflows built by different people over time. We audit what is actually used, map modules to HubSpot objects, and leave behind the fields nobody fills in.</p>
        <ul><li>Modules mapped to HubSpot objects and associations</li><li>Picklist values standardized during transformation</li><li>Workflow rules rebuilt as HubSpot automation</li><li>Unused fields retired instead of migrated</li></ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card detail" id="spreadsheets">
        <h3>Spreadsheets and legacy systems</h3>
        <p>Spreadsheet-based processes have no enforced structure, so cleanup is most of the work. We define the data model first, standardize values and formats, deduplicate, and only then import, so HubSpot starts with rules the spreadsheet never had.</p>
        <ul><li>Data model designed before import</li><li>Formats, owners, and statuses standardized</li><li>Duplicates and conflicting rows resolved</li><li>Validation rules added so the mess does not come back</li></ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
